};

type ObjectWithVariables = {
  var1?: number;
  var2?: string;
  var3?: boolean;
};

type MyObject = ObjectWithHr | ObjectWithVariables;

const objects: MyObject[] = [
  { isHr: true },
  { var1: 10, var2: "hello", var3: true },
  { var1: 20 },
  { var2: "world" },
];
```

In this example, we defined two types: `ObjectWithHr` represents an object with only the `isHr` property of type `boolean`, and `ObjectWithVariables` represents an object with optional properties `var1`, `var2`, and `var3`.

Then, we defined a union type `MyObject` that represents either an object with `isHr` or an object with the optional variables. This ensures that an object can have either the `isHr` property or the optional properties, but not both.

Finally, we created an array `objects` of type `MyObject[]` that can hold objects conforming to the defined structure. You can add objects with either the `isHr` property or the optional variables to this array.

Using this approach, TypeScript will provide type checking and prevent you from adding objects that violate the defined structure.

